+# Full study — Furstenberg's ×2, ×3 orbit theorem
+
+The frozen target says that for every irrational real `ξ`, the image in
+`ℝ/ℤ` of the two-parameter positive semigroup orbit
+`ξ · 2^m · 3^n` is dense.  Its key mathematical content is not an algebraic
+normalization but Furstenberg's rigidity/density result for the multiplicative
+semigroup generated by 2 and 3.
+
+The two generators are multiplicatively independent: an equality `2^a=3^b`
+with nonnegative exponents forces both exponents to be zero.  Furstenberg's
+theorem exploits this independence for the commuting circle endomorphisms
+`T₂(x)=2x` and `T₃(x)=3x`.  A closed invariant set for both maps is either
+finite/rational in the exceptional case or the whole circle.  An irrational
+starting point cannot have a finite orbit, so the closure of its semigroup
+orbit is the whole circle.
+
+The target quantifies only over strictly positive `m,n`.  Passing from
+nonnegative to positive exponents applies the continuous surjective map
+`x ↦ 6x` to the full semigroup orbit (equivalently, shifts both exponents by
+one), so density is preserved.  The target set-builder then spells out the
+same orbit using natural powers, multiplication in `ℕ`, coercion to `ℝ`, and
+the quotient map into `AddCircle 1`.
+
+This dossier separates three facts that are easy to conflate:
+
+1. The pinned Formal Conjectures source fixes the exact proposition and its
+   surface semantics.
+2. Its body is `by sorry`, so it supplies no acceptable proof authority.
+3. The local Lean files check the exact statement/transport/composition
+   interfaces.  A complete release additionally requires a kernel-closed
+   formalization of Furstenberg's theorem itself and an axiom census proving
+   that the root does not depend on the provider's `sorryAx`.
+
+Consequently, the content-addressed proof graph has one genuinely deep node
+(`PU-ROOT`), a definitional transport node (`PU-TRANSPORT`), and a replay/audit
+node (`PU-ROOT-CHECK`).  No prose compression is permitted to erase the
+irrationality hypothesis, the positivity of both exponents, the circle
+coercion, or the proof-authority distinction.
